our shout out tonight for one of the few  investigative reporters working in  Alaska a reporter who understood there  was a big story right in front of him  that had been ignored for years  convicted felons who were able to get  jobs as police officers in some of the  state's remote villages places where  until recently few reporters ever showed  up my name is Kyle Hopkins I'm a  reporter and the editor for the  Anchorage Daily News in Anchorage Alaska  a lot of the stories that we want to  cover and a lot of the best stories are  in these are plum villages that are hard  hard for us to get to there are all  these small communities with all these  small governments and they work in the  shadows they just do they do what they  like because there's there's no one pay  attention because their communities just  are not large enough to support on the  local journalists I found that again and  again you would see someone who had been  accused of domestic violence or you know  rape or homicide and you would also find  that that person had at what point and  sometimes still was serving as a police  officer as Hopkins discovered when he  travelled to this village above the  Arctic Circle the village of Selawik  population 850 on a November night three  years ago someone gave alcohol to 16  year old Louis Cleveland and then raped  her she died that night and while the  medical examiner called it undetermined  reasons you know they when when velasca  State Troopers investigated they found  that she had handcuff marks on her  wrists and the state police soon  determined that the village police  officer who reported the crime Brent  Norton was in fact the one who committed  the crime in an interview recorded by  state police  Norton admitted he had given her liquor  and had sex with her while she was  unconscious  Cheers  it was not his first crime Hopkins  discovered you know a simple background  check show that he already had been  convicted of who's making and he was on  probation for giving alcohol to another  underage girls so you know was had been  you know gone through the criminal  justice system was accused of and found  him guilty of a crime and was still on  the job used his used his City paycheck  to buy alcohol the thing he then gave  this girl he raped her the young victims  mother was distraught and then Hopkins  set out in the state capital to ask the  officials in charge if there were other  police officers like Norton convicted of  serious crimes but still on the job and  the response from the spray lips were a  body you know and their job is to keep  track of the stuff was you'll be have no  idea they just they didn't know they  said no one knew that led to a  full-fledged effort by Hopkins and the  Anchorage Daily News to find out why it  felt like it's the story that I had been  missing and had been right for my face  for years that we got them finally we  just finally needed to address this  that's that's really how it felt was  like you know we should have done this  years ago let's let's get this done and  just tell people what's going on and he  has he is the recipient this year of a  grant from the nonprofit pro-public to  continue his reporting as a kind of  one-man wrecking ball against apathy and  corruption I appreciate you putting a  spotlight on local reporting it's my job  nobody wants it out here so so it's nice  to talk to somebody about it so our  shout out this week - Kyle Hopkins and  his editors at the Anchorage Daily News  for devoting the time and the money to  stories that make a real difference no  matter how far at law  it takes to get them that's our report  for tonight we'll be back again next  week hope to see you then
 

